(I.) Tj T*
/F1 12.5 Tf
18.5 TL
1 0 0 1 84 688 Tm
(There is a particular hour, neither morning nor evening, when) Tj T*
(the house falls quiet and the lamp seems to draw the room a) Tj T*
(little closer. It is in this hour that a book becomes less an) Tj T*
(object and more a companion. The reader, settling in, performs a) Tj T*
(small and ancient ritual: the turning of the first page, the) Tj T*
(gentle squaring of the spine, the slow surrender of attention to) Tj T*
(another mind.) Tj T*
/F1 12.5 Tf
18.5 TL
1 0 0 1 84 550.5 Tm
(We do not read merely to be informed. If that were so, the) Tj T*
(encyclopaedia would be our only literature and the library a) Tj T*
(kind of warehouse. We read, rather, to be accompanied; to) Tj T*
(discover that a thought we believed peculiar to ourselves has) Tj T*
(been thought before, and thought more clearly, by a stranger) Tj T*
(separated from us by centuries and seas.) Tj T*
/F1 12.5 Tf
18.5 TL
1 0 0 1 84 431.5 Tm
(A good book asks little and gives much. It does not demand the) Tj T*
(bright attention of the screen, nor the constant flattery of the) Tj T*
(notification. It waits. It is patient in a way that the modern) Tj T*
(world has nearly forgotten how to be, and in waiting it teaches) Tj T*
(us, almost without our noticing, the lost art of patience) Tj T*
(itself.) Tj T*
ET

endstream
